#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134619} :activity/id #uuid "28765c46-c394-55b1-842f-82e4066f9786" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048326 :topic/id #uuid "9554dfa3-d48e-5a09-94ce-26a2a049aebc" :topic/math-academy-id 2211} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135028 :tutorial/content "To construct the standard matrix of a linear transformation $T$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ we find the images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ under $T,$ and then use those images as columns in the standard matrix.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following linear transformation: \n\n![](images/b3/b399f02949935d4a4425b19e4946a3eb47375272bb0271586c93da504ff0bd2e.svg)\n\nComputing $\\mathbf T(\\mathbf i),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 0 \\\\ 1+3\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf T(\\mathbf j),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(j) & =T\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 1 \\\\ 0+3\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for this linear transformation is ![](images/be/be0afb6703a6d5e4bcbb360297240a74f4aeec7efbbb1229c8cd01e112fa6bda.svg)\n\nIn the diagram below, we see the vectors in the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i}, \\mathbf{j} \\}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and their images $\\{ \\mathbf{i'}, \\mathbf{j'} \\}$ under the transformation.\n\n![](images/91/9189a79fe44936221e38e7c051f9f0ddd53338c80cd324ba804d61beef6c2c44.png)\n\nIn general, a linear transformation in $\\mathbb R^n$ has a standard matrix of dimensions $n \\times n,$ whose columns are the images of the basis vectors under the action of the linear transformation." :tutorial/id #uuid "840f3f2c-df1b-50e6-afae-b6c5a434f7a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3528 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5711f04c-b960-52c7-925d-15098acb82c7" :step/math-academy-id 20006 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134622}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133754 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133563 :question/id #uuid "4748324b-8edf-5fba-90e1-90683887d463" :question/math-academy-id "e-5436"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca46ab2-488a-5596-b96b-559463731556" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476951}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors Under a Given Linear Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "b4fbcf6d-62ba-54b7-9625-c8521044b1e0" :step/math-academy-id 20008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134621 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133755 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133564 :question/id #uuid "0db64243-ffa4-595b-8555-5ee369450557" :question/math-academy-id "e-5437"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2845913-5c76-57f5-a4d3-139a6513225b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479851}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Standard Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "ec1bcfe7-e63e-5e9f-a750-0f39702b4059" :step/math-academy-id 20009 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134620}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133756 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133565 :question/id #uuid "ae7884f7-abb9-56ba-b685-f0eab27ff795" :question/math-academy-id "e-5438"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b057dd5e-f80e-5d3f-84ae-b6aebd5800ea" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048203}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479898}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Standard Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given the Image of the Standard Basis"} :step/id #uuid "985fd7a6-d14e-5ae5-9b05-95876d3e50f2" :step/math-academy-id 20010 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134621}}] :activity/title "The Standard Matrix of a Linear Transformation in Terms of the Standard Basis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134047}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134813} :activity/id #uuid "77b214ca-9157-567d-b810-012cd4c3adca" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048572 :topic/id #uuid "aabe8b8c-a429-5abb-824a-40f9fdf376e0" :topic/math-academy-id 2104} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134813 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135104 :tutorial/content "Let's consider an inner product space $V$ equipped with the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle.$ \nA set of vectors \n\n$$\n{U}=\\{ \\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}\n$$\n\nis called an **orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product** if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal.\n\nFor example, let's consider the set\n\n![](images/69/69571997d87ed3f40ee7e9b0742075fbd5ee107892f6a9b4198f75f3f5a9fce3.svg)\n\nand the inner product on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y} \\rangle = x_1 y_1 + 3x_2 y_2 + 2x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nIf we denote the vectors in our set ${V}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${V}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩=0 \\\\ ⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩=0 \\\\ ⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute each inner product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(1)(2)+3(1)(2)+2(1)(-4)=2+6-8=0 & ✓ \\\\ ⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(-3)+3(1)(1)+2(1)(0)=-3+3+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ ⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(2)(-3)+3(2)(1)+2(-4)(0)=-6+6+0=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo indeed, ${V}$ is an orthogonal set." :tutorial/id #uuid "f5f69210-8732-5a94-b16b-b7de520cfc66" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5149 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "520866ac-288a-5e25-aa54-cc9335b03e13" :step/math-academy-id 32389 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134815}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134814 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133870 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133686 :question/id #uuid "cd4ebf12-7e4c-5110-872e-c381ea9b2646" :question/math-academy-id "e-8712"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555850}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Polynomials is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"} :step/id #uuid "01441c28-8b4e-5675-bb73-a2623682a7a8" :step/math-academy-id 32390 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134816}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134815 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133871 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133687 :question/id #uuid "30795f8b-9c50-5a61-9b08-385b0794c4e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8713"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050331}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430808}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Vectors Is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"} :step/id #uuid "45404b46-33b9-5949-bfc4-bc3b2e9e0898" :step/math-academy-id 32391 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134814}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134816 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133688 :question/id #uuid "dd873dd6-fdf6-50af-a080-abbb1800a096" :question/math-academy-id "e-8714"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382675}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"} :step/id #uuid "9c764926-de30-5778-b9bb-86fbaf7b9c0e" :step/math-academy-id 32392}] :activity/title "Orthogonal Sets in Inner Product Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133991}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444802} :activity/id #uuid "024faa8c-8ac3-506b-9b2c-333430674981" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048572 :topic/id #uuid "aabe8b8c-a429-5abb-824a-40f9fdf376e0" :topic/math-academy-id 2104} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444799 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7849511c-42aa-5936-80b4-cf27658d9334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470002} {:answer/id #uuid "e185f1ae-0f4d-556c-b223-3d5dfc7f46f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470003}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470003} :answer-field/id #uuid "b018c44b-9122-56d8-b47c-5ed9492b1676" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470613} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37fee277-ed91-5718-8df4-97c172646d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470004} {:answer/id #uuid "f141ac45-7382-50ba-b7cb-75b9ede77363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470005} :answer-field/id #uuid "58c60371-84d6-5ccb-aa0c-f35fe237264d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470614} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c240535-770a-5ae6-bcc0-9c4deb00ff77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470006} {:answer/id #uuid "b1721e3c-52c8-584e-98e8-7fdabdf53653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470007} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0c46d22-5599-543b-821a-3b2899b69934" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "30fad2b1-33b1-5011-baca-2c53469ec04d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99827" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=1,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0$*\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "0052f793-89f1-5c58-93a6-48098b6aceba" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444801}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444800 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90f4b8b6-9087-5496-807d-608724aa78fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470631} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5fbf91-d7c1-5e8c-871e-7c26b0ac303e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470632}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470632} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b1dfa9f-9494-59d8-bf95-2c68f20b60a5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471821} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6085786-4948-5140-96cc-351034d2e7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470633} {:answer/id #uuid "4fce3179-81d2-55bd-a37c-d2f3b28beee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470634}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470634} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffaaf373-8b01-5dda-82d5-b55d324b703d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471822} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fecb31b-eea1-5984-a868-c589f3e59e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470635} {:answer/id #uuid "0b89d20b-e663-5580-8552-4bead320d1f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470636}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470635} :answer-field/id #uuid "a51c31a2-df0f-5d9f-bff0-d622cce2a150" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb972e63-3e46-543b-9a40-0120a6183656" :question/math-academy-id "q-100120" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t-4,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2-t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=1-t.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(1)q(1)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t-4,1-t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t-4){|}_{1}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{1}+(2t-4){|}_{2}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(1)-4)\\cdot (1-1)+(2(2)-4)\\cdot (1-2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2-t,1-t⟩ \\\\  & =(2-t){|}_{1}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{1}+(2-t){|}_{2}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (1-1)+(2-2)\\cdot (1-2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t-4,2-t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t-4){|}_{1}\\cdot (2-t){|}_{1}+(2t-4){|}_{2}\\cdot (2-t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(1)-4)\\cdot (2-1)+(2(2)-4)\\cdot (2-2) \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "246bcb3d-ab36-5471-897e-d2a54e64c4f2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444803}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444801 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237782 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc79c40-802c-5928-96bd-62b5abc8ad53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461159} {:answer/id #uuid "624f3dda-272a-59f4-81c4-afdc89b1b569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461160} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1795204-45a0-5ae2-b1e0-fe24f1661e9a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462333} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d409008c-3927-5c03-9206-ddde6c3ce4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461161} {:answer/id #uuid "1a4f7f7d-e705-5bea-b85e-15ecc20591e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461162} :answer-field/id #uuid "75ba454e-4199-5bbb-a3a5-a8496e22c83d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462334} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ce821b-8ac0-57db-b8d9-ccfbcbc7d8fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461163} {:answer/id #uuid "3128022b-3800-59d4-aea7-1dbf26659f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461164}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461163} :answer-field/id #uuid "104242d5-dc4e-5e24-ac69-a557c354d5fa" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0e262e4d-a912-58d1-aa39-b29d3810a43d" :question/math-academy-id "q-100119" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-2,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t+2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2t+4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-2)q(-2)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,t+2⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{-2}\\cdot (t+2){|}_{-2}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (t+2){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2-2)\\cdot ((-2)+2)+(2-2)\\cdot (2+2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,2t+4⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{-2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{-2}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2-2)\\cdot (2(-2)+4)+(2-2)\\cdot (2(2)+4) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t+2,2t+4⟩ \\\\  & =(t+2){|}_{-2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{-2}+(t+2){|}_{2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{2} \\\\  & =((-2)+2)\\cdot (2(-2)+4)+(2+2)\\cdot (2(2)+4) \\\\  & =32\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "7719daea-6d1b-5000-8b05-8e73d08e6429" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444800}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444802 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcbe8ed8-a2e7-59a7-9dcd-ca895d94f772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469996} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2456c5-a5b0-5c29-a226-52a11f29ba70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469997}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469997} :answer-field/id #uuid "245ca49b-3ef1-5ba6-9c92-331b631ef8af" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470610} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "862656ec-c41e-53ed-b5b1-593d83f896af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469998} {:answer/id #uuid "1101d9b7-2ce3-5a80-8a13-ab7fef2e907e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469999}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469998} :answer-field/id #uuid "c906d66a-475e-595f-a93e-72e736687fd0" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470611} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9d0153f-7d93-5c51-a0b3-bdfb99253f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470000} {:answer/id #uuid "a378038d-21ed-5b5c-81b1-271985e61763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470000} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f4e14fd-7f71-596e-94da-74a2248a0192" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5c3132dd-8104-5475-a04a-6ba3130fe419" :question/math-academy-id "q-99822" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,{f}_{3}(t)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[0,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{1},{f}_{2}⟩=0.$*\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,1⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,1⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡\\alpha =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\alpha )}{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[t-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}]{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\pi -\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{2}-(0-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\pi -0-(0-0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{2}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{2}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "7f2a99f1-09d3-5682-bc31-b6d8c58eeab4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444799}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444803 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380406 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38977e25-5701-59b4-acd7-c07e28c73e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470637} {:answer/id #uuid "795d2da5-60e6-5507-9355-3921ddc95792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470638}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470638} :answer-field/id #uuid "c745d006-d5de-5a2f-9e45-1c00146eae2a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471824} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4846a1f4-412a-515c-8ef9-ed21e5470f60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470639} {:answer/id #uuid "e6594a2d-fafd-5fa9-8390-bc86bc6736e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470639} :answer-field/id #uuid "4afd9177-d9a1-52e6-9b31-5287eb0e1f1b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471825} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40c96296-fbc5-5ab2-9c9f-253e362d1483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470641} {:answer/id #uuid "95e9e5ba-829c-5853-bb9d-a29fdf02c6fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470642}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470641} :answer-field/id #uuid "54703d9d-be5e-5e7e-ab6d-caae8559077f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "39aa7418-1a3f-5a9e-9642-6c49cf6dc36a" :question/math-academy-id "q-100129" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t+2,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t-2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=t+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t+2,t-2⟩ \\\\  & =(2t+2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t-2){|}_{-1}+(2t+2){|}_{2}\\cdot (t-2){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(-1)+2)\\cdot ((-1)-2)+(2(2)+2)\\cdot (2-2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{2} \\\\  & =((-1)-2)\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(2-2)\\cdot (2+1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t+2,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(2t+2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(2t+2){|}_{2}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(2(-1)+2)\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(2(2)+2)\\cdot (2+1) \\\\  & =18\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "f46df5aa-3941-5c4e-8920-7488094bbef3"}] :activity/title "Orthogonal Sets in Inner Product Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444798}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518224} :activity/id #uuid "27c4f973-8cd9-5bf9-b1b9-5d95669edfef" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048572 :topic/id #uuid "aabe8b8c-a429-5abb-824a-40f9fdf376e0" :topic/math-academy-id 2104} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518224 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5a7789d-bf18-52bd-bc9b-301af5c6d5fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469984} {:answer/id #uuid "0055086d-aa25-5fda-b4af-c9666a64eb04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469985}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469985} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5b78874-d300-5fa5-9795-d39ff2596e54" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470604} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcbf6b25-801c-5c97-8c47-fa6594f98096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469986} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1f59b9-0ffa-5059-8021-bd1050c6ae27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469987}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469986} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1d427c5-5b56-5cfe-b771-f93205be779a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470605} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d15ce6-683e-5205-83b1-3ef4c0c45fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469988} {:answer/id #uuid "01c3bb42-0a0e-557a-a08c-494e7ee85d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469989}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469989} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8cf17fc-2cba-51b8-b0bf-47f1618c05ee" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "689e4d4d-2277-5c62-9cd6-9887b81429d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-99656" :question/problem "$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **II.** ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **III.** $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(1)(4)+2(1)(-1)+2(-1)(1) \\\\  & =4-2-2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(4)(0)+2(-1)(1)+2(1)(1) \\\\  & =0-2+2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(0)+2(1)(1)+2(-1)(1) \\\\  & =0+2-2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "068d6b66-d519-5161-9eb3-7efaee833625" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518316}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518233 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b37083a7-aaaa-5a2a-973a-5c9ff944dfe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470032} {:answer/id #uuid "aade6615-cdec-5bb3-9ebc-b52ba6498acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470033}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470033} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ee6465c-b492-5dcd-9a5b-72f46a3a5104" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470628} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dcfb967-51e5-5257-b6ea-f6b21931da14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470034} {:answer/id #uuid "d6927137-4c46-5fd0-a3de-bc83e11cc13a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470035}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470034} :answer-field/id #uuid "f55811ff-e66f-5a0e-812a-3b75349cc59e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470629} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ec93396-6ade-5b54-b2dc-4c3f0308a1a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470036} {:answer/id #uuid "42e1db1a-4eb1-5170-b19b-b7d378bf5855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470037}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186470036} :answer-field/id #uuid "8547b10e-8dd7-5314-9138-e70dea301350" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d313e454-e9a2-5532-b1b1-37c4d7cbbe80" :question/math-academy-id "q-99951" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{2}(t)=t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,2]$ of continuous functions over $[0,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **II.** ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **III.** $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨{e}^{t},t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}(t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-t){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-2-(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{e}^{t},{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "288cbc9e-dc23-58e1-8dd2-9368f609a42d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518298}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518288 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382675 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bb3f792-8daa-5481-90a3-72c22e1e0cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471815} {:answer/id #uuid "37bf6595-efed-5e05-9eea-c39ccace55ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471816}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471815} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef3c0786-1837-57ae-b61a-5fcc2a1ae0da" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472413} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82d9a59f-6071-55e9-ba83-81a93266ba78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471817} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4ccf74-dfd0-5457-ae1d-2f4b1376f55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471818} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4772d99-ef37-508b-be2b-5bf2236274df" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472414} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "983ffe32-ea8e-5549-a804-3f8a808db229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471819} {:answer/id #uuid "9b9010c5-b935-59a9-aa88-3c4656c8a125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471820}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471819} :answer-field/id #uuid "9241636c-59e4-57aa-90d4-9f47ca3f6f1e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "866dfd7d-0156-5915-b7a2-48bb304694df" :question/math-academy-id "q-99949" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi /2,\\pi /2]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi /2,\\pi /2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **II.** ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **III.** $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ differentiating we have $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$ Hence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{sin}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "b9cfa362-8135-55c9-a260-2d92a37de08c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518233}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237781 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db20fdb5-b211-5717-8065-e53a2737e52f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461153} {:answer/id #uuid "6656a7b8-c8ea-5900-b91f-827cf9160375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461154}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461154} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e43ba6a-40ab-5c1a-b8c8-a8858483f612" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462330} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20ed73bc-5b6f-5ab3-960f-9b7f15c8d785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461155} {:answer/id #uuid "9793f44e-45c4-5a51-9746-6e6a99a83a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461156}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461156} :answer-field/id #uuid "ded516f2-2e1d-5b1d-9ede-938fe1e44d44" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462331} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb2b82bd-c25b-51f4-bfaf-3cdcb7a1a58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461157} {:answer/id #uuid "3b12847b-cc3a-5250-b4d9-33f8b9d8c07f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461158}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461157} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebb5c838-cff5-5fc9-b5c7-6f2cc94f2a50" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ea1a91d4-93f7-5e8d-b2ce-c7694a823cec" :question/math-academy-id "q-100114" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t+1,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=3t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=t+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(-1)q(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **II.** ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **III.** $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t+1,3t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t+1){|}_{0}\\cdot (3t){|}_{0}+(2t+1){|}_{-1}\\cdot (3t){|}_{-1} \\\\  & =(2(0)+1)\\cdot (3(0))+(2(-1)+1)\\cdot (3(-1)) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨3t,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(3t){|}_{0}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{0}+(3t){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1} \\\\  & =(3(0))\\cdot (0+1)+(3(-1))\\cdot ((-1)+1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "d30ded70-89ee-555a-8552-22899b8ddbfa"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518316 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15066878-30bb-508c-9db7-e8b10b72c732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133453} {:answer/id #uuid "1c2b7f86-e92f-5e2c-8860-4730a35f5501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133454} {:answer/id #uuid "29f32be0-6e29-59ac-a011-e3b3dd051fee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133455} {:answer/id #uuid "73574aac-cbae-5bc4-ab25-5412b8255db1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133456} {:answer/id #uuid "7a14430d-daac-5eb0-95da-dfa439a6fb71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133457}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133456} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e7eb080-b1fd-507d-8288-fb67ef87f7d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a07c5a93-5161-56bc-aa10-7d11733928e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-99808" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)={t}^{2},\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{3},\\,{f}_{3}(t)=t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{1},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{2},{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{6}}{6}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{6}}{6}-\\frac{(-1)^{6}}{6} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{t}^{3},t⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{3}\\cdot t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{(-1)^{5}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{2}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{2}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "f43963c8-38c4-5336-a8cf-309c2c95dbe1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518288}}] :activity/title "Orthogonal Sets in Inner Product Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517479}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134701} :activity/id #uuid "c184b7bf-d80e-522a-9bde-709e111cc316" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049845 :topic/id #uuid "183bab17-91bb-560a-b6e6-758a6cc0dfcd" :topic/math-academy-id 2168} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134701 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135061 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the least-squares problem for the system\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe can use QR factorization to construct the least-squares solution of this system. Recall that finding the QR factorization of $A$ means expressing $A$ in the form\n\n$$\nA = QR,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $Q$ has orthonormal columns, and\n\n- $R$ is an upper triangular matrix with positive entries on its main diagonal.\n\nTo see how QR factorization can be used to solve a least-squares problem, we first recall that the least-squares solution satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\nA^T A \\mathbf{x} = A^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A = QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, we have $Q^T Q = I.$ Therefore, the above equation becomes\n\n$$\nR^TR\\mathbf{x} = R^TQ^T\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSince $R^T$ is invertible, pre-multiplying our equation by $(R^T)^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nR \\mathbf{x} = Q^T \\mathbf{b}.  \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution $\\hat{\\mathbf x}$ can now be found by solving this equation using back-substitution or row operations. This is usually the fastest way.\n\nIf we want an explicit formula for the least-squares solution, then we note that since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying $(\\ast)$ by $R^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = R^{-1} Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution is unique if $A$ has linearly independent columns." :tutorial/id #uuid "7a9501fc-fdbc-5905-ad31-e3c6c2767a33" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3639 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "733946c8-bb91-5523-9be6-6e334aa51df6" :step/math-academy-id 20276 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134887}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134702 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133803 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133612 :question/id #uuid "5fb2571c-2ab9-5fe5-9454-f9d7b0e89854" :question/math-academy-id "e-5592"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d177b305-cdd1-5f91-b9d5-90623e52ad10" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510447}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given a QR Factorization"} :step/id #uuid "eeb32ce1-92f7-5c9a-8d57-a6fb5f276cdf" :step/math-academy-id 20278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134887 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133906 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133719 :question/id #uuid "d805b370-4377-5101-8ac7-3d30e11156ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-9917"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7d226d3-f7d3-574f-94f9-a532ec99b0ac" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050130}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510454}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Expression for a Least-Squares Solution in Terms of the Matrices Q and R"} :step/id #uuid "eab53e14-a261-5ebb-9ee3-6d39662a78cf" :step/math-academy-id 37297 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134889}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134889 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135143 :tutorial/content "Let's find the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where $A = QR,$ and\n![](images/4e/4e2433312f3a424aa161d5aeec63df38787c43435a9c2af44ba6b3d63f9ef065.svg)\n\nPreviously, we showed that the normal equation corresponding to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where $A=QR,$ can be reduced to\n\n$$\nR \\mathbf{x} = Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our matrices into this equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{2} &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4(2) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =10 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ is ![](images/72/723835f5ecb612e7d79c29ada9cc05241ffd4f233e29c9ed9a643994580a8c36.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "5fcac262-421e-57b7-b038-713427320ff4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6150 :tutorial/title "Using QR Factorization to Solve a Least-Squares Problem"} :step/id #uuid "f3959285-3101-555f-96a2-bd9e95bfe12e" :step/math-academy-id 37330 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134702}}] :activity/title "Finding a Least-Squares Solution Using QR Factorization" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134028}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410813} :activity/id #uuid "9b29a2c1-0e8f-51a5-8bb4-88c6d8d5a680" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049845 :topic/id #uuid "183bab17-91bb-560a-b6e6-758a6cc0dfcd" :topic/math-academy-id 2168} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39d0e813-26d6-5597-ad13-db18f30203df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${A}^{T}$ and $b$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410786} {:answer/id #uuid "4f27a535-7b11-525f-9c56-8b5ab7bcd664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${R}^{-1}$ and $b$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410787} {:answer/id #uuid "9cef819e-2e48-567b-8a8f-3202e6986c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${b}^{T}$ and $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410793} {:answer/id #uuid "e0801752-ba2f-56ca-be80-5bf37502d69c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${b}^{T}$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410800} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e67057-98e7-5f3b-a5f1-858830ac094c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$R$ and $b$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410801}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410787} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8574fe5-c6b2-5e0e-b6b5-71b14a1ca898" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6c899a77-435a-5aa3-92d4-0dc3a7b627aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-158080" :question/problem "$$\nx=\\,{Q}^{T}\\,\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nThen, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by ${R}^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b \\\\ x & ={R}^{-1}\\,\\,{Q}^{T}\\,\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are ${R}^{-1}$ and $b.$"} :step/id #uuid "6b34aecb-fc33-591a-beae-813382c608f3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410815}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410813 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f55e0b-2bfd-5324-bb60-ef44a8ce8695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$R$ and ${b}^{T}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410778} {:answer/id #uuid "0acc060c-c80f-5318-9ceb-31102a3b5205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${R}^{-1}$ and $b$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410780} {:answer/id #uuid "accbb9fd-1e2b-5eb4-9d55-1c270308aa87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${R}^{-1}$ and $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410796} {:answer/id #uuid "d56e2344-c38a-5d60-89d9-ddd844e5f790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$R$ and $b$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410798} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d265c7-1332-5802-b568-b47af3ef47f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${R}^{T}$ and $b$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410802}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410780} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d57014e-b23a-5701-a76a-a3703de7149c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "285a0657-649f-57c4-8ca1-f228238070d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-139773" :question/problem "$$\nx=\\,{Q}^{T}\\,\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nThen, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by ${R}^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b \\\\ x & ={R}^{-1}\\,\\,{Q}^{T}\\,\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are ${R}^{-1}$ and $b.$"} :step/id #uuid "70103913-067c-52aa-850c-673f2ce5ac0f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410816}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410814 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410810 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12a0bfb2-9ec4-5669-b98c-557ad2a9bc4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410782} {:answer/id #uuid "2af5f523-a673-51cf-8e9d-4a4edaf229e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410785} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc6960e-7f53-5f78-93da-de1e72ff8ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410789} {:answer/id #uuid "a27e8c2d-50df-58c3-85c9-4e38ce8d2589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410794} {:answer/id #uuid "be9612fa-59cc-5f2d-8433-f16960b6ff97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410797}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410794} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a8d53f7-b5fc-5ce6-9a9c-54a7385b4356" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6e2326cd-c939-5766-9e1e-50b935159886" :question/math-academy-id "q-157095" :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{14} & -3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{14} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & -3\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{14}{x}_{2} &=-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{14}(0) & =-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{14}{x}_{1} & =-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "8ef06eb7-a320-54c5-a67e-7c28d93f3d07" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410812}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410815 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410808 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09435933-4c28-52dd-8678-41dcf0848333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410779} {:answer/id #uuid "24985731-b6b9-5e8d-820b-1f13827d0949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(R-m\\cdot I)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410783} {:answer/id #uuid "50d2e581-451b-5360-b4da-e26396ea468e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(R-I)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410788} {:answer/id #uuid "7dceedd7-4b1f-5a60-b090-4dc26f3702e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410790} {:answer/id #uuid "91cae48a-5ff2-5a13-a3b1-20c945ab4731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{T}R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410792}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410790} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eb41613-58b3-5878-8f3f-4404da6faf4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4e50c9b4-d5fe-527c-b554-c5ae45ba5355" :question/math-academy-id "q-158081" :question/problem "$$\nx=\\,{Q}^{T}\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ What is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nThen, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by ${R}^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b \\\\ x & ={R}^{-1}\\,\\,{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing part of the equation is ${R}^{-1}.$"} :step/id #uuid "d783bc27-1d4d-5fd3-a3db-1a036831d9dd"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410816 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cfa9745-27c9-5c69-9259-9f33cdd30cbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410781} {:answer/id #uuid "290a80b2-d833-5e18-ba11-922da75fadbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410784} {:answer/id #uuid "84becdeb-7cb6-51f9-8247-37058f839cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410791} {:answer/id #uuid "ab331c8e-8ec8-5a70-b8c2-e3efcd32bf48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410795} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a2f5a3-da1b-5892-8fa1-25b99c4d869f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410799}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410795} :answer-field/id #uuid "44672c62-c008-5400-a940-292eccf518a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c8892a71-2970-5d65-891c-76f1124825ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-139796" :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & -1 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2} &=38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=-10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-5.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-4\\sqrt{2}(-5) & =38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1} & =18\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "de997394-83f9-5ca3-92c7-b205bedc19d1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410814}}] :activity/title "Finding a Least-Squares Solution Using QR Factorization" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410777}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 1