herefore, the missing part of the equation is ${Q}^{T}.$"} :step/id #uuid "e98b958c-ff8a-5dac-9009-16dbadd67c04" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510559}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510559 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2312d1c9-ae68-5003-ac7e-7414fd467ee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509761} {:answer/id #uuid "425e926e-f6d7-5135-b061-818402d0d1d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509828} {:answer/id #uuid "73a71808-3fa5-573e-b8b1-b19de5e75149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{b}^{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509931} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ae5c2d-37b1-5cab-9352-7357b57e7f85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{b}{‖b‖}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510048} {:answer/id #uuid "da0250e9-1d30-5606-9e21-317e8cba1e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{A}^{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510153}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509761} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4202d35-11cb-528d-8f7b-8baa57144892" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cb3d3dae-b723-59f7-8d1b-568e2e8664df" :question/math-academy-id "q-139772" :question/problem "$$\nx={R}^{-1}{Q}^{T}\\,\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ What is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nThen, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by ${R}^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b \\\\ x & ={R}^{-1}{Q}^{T}\\,\\,b\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing part of the equation is $b.$"} :step/id #uuid "eeaed52a-85d1-5761-bbe1-bcc1658b24ad" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510562}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d72c52-be81-5a78-89ce-5f13fcd20525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509726} {:answer/id #uuid "39ecad14-1c0a-5aad-bfee-718224c4fd70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509813} {:answer/id #uuid "782b8fc8-baa5-5617-a0d8-b51e87778593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509943} {:answer/id #uuid "7ccbb9de-1d05-5669-a825-c1565d374612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509956} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ba3a48-87bd-5410-a677-49fe3a6248f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510130}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509956} :answer-field/id #uuid "551c0677-6855-511f-8997-47d81f6ef2d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "02febb14-e263-5cc1-8053-2e425b9d6cc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-139783" :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,R=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is orthogonal, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=-11 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4(5) & =-11 \\\\ 3{x}_{1} & =9 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$"} :step/id #uuid "f8407afd-1563-5fa7-a7c0-218f08f7c276" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510486}}] :activity/title "Finding a Least-Squares Solution Using QR Factorization" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509688}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162849} :activity/id #uuid "22c8da45-56d1-5645-85df-aa94cccb29d8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049849 :topic/id #uuid "1887f465-a1b8-5370-82f6-80e02b9d153a" :topic/math-academy-id 3120} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162849 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163224 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $\\mathbb{R}.$ The **spectral theorem** states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- The eigenvectors corresponding to *different* eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable." :tutorial/id #uuid "5ecbbdd0-25a0-52ee-8f0c-987dcd8ba584" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4882 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "84b2f14e-f6c7-5b6c-b6d7-19d4d7d71f71" :step/math-academy-id 30533 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162013 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161805 :question/id #uuid "6ae71f41-133f-50b6-a286-20ae7e2c3a9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8291"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c67d532-acd9-5231-a27f-d39084863e01" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048493}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544892}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Spectral Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "95f72e96-f84b-5b21-a070-253638be57a8" :step/math-academy-id 30970 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163243 :tutorial/content "We can write an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix $A$ in terms of a sum of products of its eigenvalues and eigenvectors.\n\nSpecifically, if $\\mathbf{u}_i$ denotes an orthonormal eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_i$ for $i=1,\\ldots, n$, then\n\n$$\nA=\\sum_{i=1}^n \\lambda_i \\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T.\n$$\n\nExpressing a symmetric matrix $A$ in this way is called the **spectral decomposition** of $A.$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the spectral decomposition of the $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/40/408e7471e4ef788758d423af97f5683186f79192fd8933c350d45bb87f089875.svg)\n\nThe eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1 = 6$ and $\\lambda_2 = -4,$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are\n\n![](images/0c/0c22f7dd97e83c358cb3cef1fcc2db902d908140109334a8cf1c22b35b29c6d2.svg)\n\nThe spectral decomposition of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA =\\sum_{i=1}^2 \\lambda_i \\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf u_1 \\mathbf u_1^T + \\lambda_2 \\mathbf u_2 \\mathbf u_2^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_1$ and $\\mathbf u_2$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spectral decomposition of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{6}_{⏟}}_{{\\lambda }_{1}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{10} & \\frac{3}{10} \\\\ \\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{9}{10}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}}+{{(-4)}_{⏟}}_{{\\lambda }_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{10} & -\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this decomposition, the matrix $\\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T$ is the orthogonal projection matrix that maps any $\\mathbf x \\in \\mathbb R^2$ onto the subspace spanned by $\\mathbf u_i.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a90957e3-e4b9-548d-930b-e060c6b63859" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4973 :tutorial/title "The Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "8df507ec-4d53-59f1-b941-07907cd14b6f" :step/math-academy-id 30971 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162014 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161806 :question/id #uuid "d185ef42-c177-5217-99b7-b42d800f3385" :question/math-academy-id "e-8292"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84207049-12f7-514c-bfa2-51bce8631b41" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049489}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511494}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Projection Matrix That Orthogonally Projects a Vector to the Subspace Spanned by an Eigenvector"} :step/id #uuid "2a1d5d86-333c-5361-87ec-fd7766797988" :step/math-academy-id 30972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162015 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161807 :question/id #uuid "65b23c21-528f-5df7-a00c-8e2dce041276" :question/math-academy-id "e-8293"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "785b09aa-1b82-5021-8b10-271a063cb916" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511495}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "86248bdd-6a75-54cd-8196-8b333a64cac5" :step/math-academy-id 30973 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162904}}] :activity/title "The Spectral Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162216}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407555} :activity/id #uuid "cb802846-7e96-5e29-a7fd-43a85cc938d8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049849 :topic/id #uuid "1887f465-a1b8-5370-82f6-80e02b9d153a" :topic/math-academy-id 3120} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "237e9827-741e-554d-b4bd-a580c12832ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407524} {:answer/id #uuid "637ff16c-2a36-5623-9258-1f3130b80075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407529} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8f4850-6613-5ef7-a355-f12825aba902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407532} {:answer/id #uuid "bea7dec3-53c9-52fb-9b61-30210ef0b89a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407538} {:answer/id #uuid "d23ed5d0-4d06-561d-902d-1213f1013134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407539}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407524} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b7eee37-4841-5d7e-83f1-ad47a007de82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "69f0b9bd-9336-5c1c-91e3-bf72b33b1333" :question/math-academy-id "q-140847" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+8\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+8\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "07386288-c5f3-55df-b73a-99cec25cb7d6"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162646 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1556dc1b-0c8b-54e0-a340-18371f592ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407520} {:answer/id #uuid "286d06a8-1050-59ed-a665-558d840c2aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407525} {:answer/id #uuid "d6effcc8-e643-5b20-9090-b3855faacab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407541} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f4f75f-447a-5e0c-a1d6-feae985a30a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407543} {:answer/id #uuid "f93a6cf9-78b6-546d-ba88-361fc48a056a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407544}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407544} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebade115-5b0c-52d7-94cc-8ec9e5babad9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11bdef24-ff68-513b-bfa6-5f2fb8c78b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-94768" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2d35b336-2654-53f7-8116-4689735d233e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407557}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407556 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16877fa0-d3a3-5da8-ba77-c7e71244a7c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407521} {:answer/id #uuid "6d264661-257d-5c8a-8684-122b5c0a6807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407531} {:answer/id #uuid "9555f6b0-dd96-561e-b956-a28b10613e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407535} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0b779d-af50-58f8-97aa-dd75027108dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407536} {:answer/id #uuid "d6231cbd-de79-555a-b056-b8008595ed25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407531} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1092e23-b9a3-5556-b0d1-0745f95be4b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7e3f233e-913d-50cf-8912-1c6832afca12" :question/math-academy-id "q-95725" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-65 & 78 \\\\ 78 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5fc71f89-cc9a-549d-9f1f-b3a2081e4a63" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407554}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d327dec-d42a-581c-8707-926d0b6893a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407522} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3ec327-8643-5dfe-8735-8c0774db03ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407526} {:answer/id #uuid "799755e8-7ecf-5b56-8460-da2805b858a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407533} {:answer/id #uuid "95054c16-a500-55cc-9c6d-5dc257b93966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407534} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b2cd88-0c1c-5067-96b8-f34a70eda38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407542} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb85388f-97d4-5b7a-9384-bb45d3a9d42b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9d231abd-db3d-5d11-9253-820e7eb51742" :question/math-academy-id "q-95712" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 18\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 18\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 16 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{17}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "61132d09-e84e-51b9-b83a-aedca27c1563" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407558}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f090535-e10a-5c59-9a51-2d2aae1cbc9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407523} {:answer/id #uuid "3f43beb7-1a8e-5d63-bf78-07894e8c2c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407527} {:answer/id #uuid "5613d7c8-bedb-54d2-be91-7e80b596eb5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407528} {:answer/id #uuid "694df5b3-0ed3-5486-8f0d-63bf6d2b9e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407530} {:answer/id #uuid "bc550768-52c7-5626-83cb-470760bd86c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407537}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407523} :answer-field/id #uuid "a49b921e-a620-50dc-993e-f6b1a37ca3f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b502656e-ef35-5f6c-921c-ae3a8ea1f2a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-95721" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-6,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-25 & 10 \\\\ 10 & -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "639ff62f-ac48-56c2-bf2b-580bf31ccf01" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407556}}] :activity/title "The Spectral Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407519}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495258} :activity/id #uuid "f7834130-0260-5c43-bf45-c2988a6f854e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049849 :topic/id #uuid "1887f465-a1b8-5370-82f6-80e02b9d153a" :topic/math-academy-id 3120} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495205 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a866929-0beb-5a32-8959-34966c6e6ad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494370} {:answer/id #uuid "28cc6daa-99e2-5439-a52d-8c74f6f8d55b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494393} {:answer/id #uuid "7d73673d-5635-5b93-8b05-960db3e3f68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494590} {:answer/id #uuid "891a910a-dee7-5f7a-b066-db75538859fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494623} {:answer/id #uuid "fec25ec0-ae75-52d4-992b-cbf821a92df5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494915}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494915} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f1f6569-2671-58ca-aa36-0fefdf95c894" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f8b0f486-8442-538c-933a-d85ad556ff51" :question/math-academy-id "q-95716" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 17\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$ Computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 17\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 16 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=5.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "67e20c48-efe2-5fd4-8d06-cb1125f84345" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495243}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495243 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddb77fdb-9e1a-59d2-a520-e52d80297bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378067} {:answer/id #uuid "20a24a23-34cd-59e2-919c-ffa350d8078c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378068} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f8cd17-d0e2-5147-8618-30de79a463fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378069} {:answer/id #uuid "5649503f-0459-5e9f-9fcf-d4672d0737b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378070} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6a63c9-7482-5e58-94cf-dffcb3862ae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378071}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378069} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ed15ba3-7536-51e1-aa4c-32ec9da0175e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e3527660-7336-5854-9fe8-3ed70b1dd1cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-95723" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-40 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 40\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b38fd098-3465-5c13-8a12-93f1b35b5090" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495260}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495253 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162662 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07faab27-15f7-5312-8b38-59ad02f72521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -25\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494338} {:answer/id #uuid "2f44ec68-5673-5d17-a202-807989a0d5ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -25\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494410} {:answer/id #uuid "6c89eafa-421f-5962-9af4-946ba03a1964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494540} {:answer/id #uuid "8b205150-ffac-59f3-b8f8-30947cc22faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494635} {:answer/id #uuid "9d276ed0-9aab-5f35-92de-cd40b3be996e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494678} :answer-field/id #uuid "27a49c53-38b7-5dbb-b5f4-f47add37c5d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "28c27a06-cd3b-5e3d-a19e-4b46b9bccd18" :question/math-academy-id "q-95714" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-10$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 15\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-10.$ Computing $A-(-10)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+10I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 15\\end{bmatrix}+10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 25\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 25 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-5{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{26}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cd9e99be-c8ca-50d9-aacd-09344ae9dae2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495205}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495258 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162647 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9efaaf0-779f-5a72-aa61-4761f6295dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366582} {:answer/id #uuid "5d20e65d-8c96-5a7e-8fea-9024d97dc82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366583} {:answer/id #uuid "d2283d99-cd32-55e5-9365-da3163600171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366584} {:answer/id #uuid "1172809a-fec9-5ba9-a260-4a0f08543d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366585} {:answer/id #uuid "d89d3c9b-2928-5750-a596-9fb110e7f349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366586} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4531768-c85a-5927-b9ea-dd6e8018f412" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "48eccd0e-cb0a-5561-99a4-875f56fc9de1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94769" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-8)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-8)\\cdot \\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}(9\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-8)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}(9\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}16 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-8)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}136 & -68 \\\\ -68 & -119\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d7e437ea-03ba-522c-8c2f-e2da5dacae70" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495253}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495260 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d17ddb9-9738-5247-980d-6346f34ca3d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395068} {:answer/id #uuid "9112c274-741d-5926-a7e8-28550395e3fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395069} {:answer/id #uuid "a6833e0f-d872-5f84-8fad-2cd587942a79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395070} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a1eeb9-2422-526b-996e-3116b894cad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395071} {:answer/id #uuid "bb3216f5-0141-54bf-b967-4b7d4a78081b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395072}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395068} :answer-field/id #uuid "da07241e-4465-5487-a804-a1f93208770d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4f741f54-e9ba-55b4-9c81-df73745d51c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-95724" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dc9cbb5e-30fb-5f9a-8c8a-71b1f6f7926c"}] :activity/title "The Spectral Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494318}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511632} :activity/id #uuid "b98685f7-3920-523a-8d3c-632e830a9d7d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049849 :topic/id #uuid "1887f465-a1b8-5370-82f6-80e02b9d153a" :topic/math-academy-id 3120} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f090535-e10a-5c59-9a51-2d2aae1cbc9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407523} {:answer/id #uuid "3f43beb7-1a8e-5d63-bf78-07894e8c2c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407527} {:answer/id #uuid "5613d7c8-bedb-54d2-be91-7e80b596eb5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407528} {:answer/id #uuid "694df5b3-0ed3-5486-8f0d-63bf6d2b9e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407530} {:answer/id #uuid "bc550768-52c7-5626-83cb-470760bd86c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407537}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407523} :answer-field/id #uuid "a49b921e-a620-50dc-993e-f6b1a37ca3f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b502656e-ef35-5f6c-921c-ae3a8ea1f2a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-95721" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-6,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-25 & 10 \\\\ 10 & -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3dcce81f-cc42-5948-b429-8ba4a11557f3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511622}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16877fa0-d3a3-5da8-ba77-c7e71244a7c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407521} {:answer/id #uuid "6d264661-257d-5c8a-8684-122b5c0a6807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407531} {:answer/id #uuid "9555f6b0-dd96-561e-b956-a28b10613e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407535} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0b779d-af50-58f8-97aa-dd75027108dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407536} {:answer/id #uuid "d6231cbd-de79-555a-b056-b8008595ed25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407531} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1092e23-b9a3-5556-b0d1-0745f95be4b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7e3f233e-913d-50cf-8912-1c6832afca12" :question/math-academy-id "q-95725" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-65 & 78 \\\\ 78 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "55f4143a-4a93-5e8d-b696-93cfb4a2e6c1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511668}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162646 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1556dc1b-0c8b-54e0-a340-18371f592ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407520} {:answer/id #uuid "286d06a8-1050-59ed-a665-558d840c2aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407525} {:answer/id #uuid "d6effcc8-e643-5b20-9090-b3855faacab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407541} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f4f75f-447a-5e0c-a1d6-feae985a30a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407543} {:answer/id #uuid "f93a6cf9-78b6-546d-ba88-361fc48a056a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407544}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407544} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebade115-5b0c-52d7-94cc-8ec9e5babad9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11bdef24-ff68-513b-bfa6-5f2fb8c78b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-94768" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7f7d44ce-dbe3-5404-92b7-3a9a5ceebe0e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511668 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a9ba5aa-7ff7-5a13-8435-4ae5f003f1ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510800} {:answer/id #uuid "253fb856-2658-5841-900d-3d095bc94cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510861} {:answer/id #uuid "4986bc0c-e982-51ac-aecd-3824f87d8c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510945} {:answer/id #uuid "631ec9ac-1a1a-52f7-bf42-8397061beb8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510992} {:answer/id #uuid "ea35d22f-50f1-5cf4-aa38-4adac074dc4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511270}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511270} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4cd93bf-3a8c-5f18-b352-8eff8b26fc90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "524b5d0d-afb1-56e2-9f18-7dc1697e2495" :question/math-academy-id "q-95728" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((3)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -10 \\\\ -10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d68ef3a3-b3f5-5888-8b3c-31c4716078a7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a0018a6-7dae-54cf-88b4-02338b8fff5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510914} {:answer/id #uuid "a218fed0-75a7-50d9-8f60-c86aeaebfd23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511121} {:answer/id #uuid "a24957bf-c3bd-5b14-b0b3-55b5967d855a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511123} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5b03f7-2a8e-557c-abde-00dafe7a50cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511173} {:answer/id #uuid "e533b56d-0765-5385-b5e3-a0e0dfcc5a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511264}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510914} :answer-field/id #uuid "29cd7cba-3362-549f-8e3e-97da0f1c1325" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4d6df4b5-e301-58fd-845a-847c7a249d39" :question/math-academy-id "q-140849" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f8df9c63-69f3-5acb-8e84-5b34d208def1"}] :activity/title "The Spectral Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510766}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544933} :activity/id #uuid "0ee7b968-1996-5dc0-bce6-494a4e054d04" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049849 :topic/id #uuid "1887f465-a1b8-5370-82f6-80e02b9d153a" :topic/math-academy-id 3120} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb7dde31-bde1-58e8-ad71-ec6b2e8b2319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451311} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca9aaad-54c8-5fad-9a03-14113c01dc8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451312}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451311} :answer-field/id #uuid "e741fb79-46a4-593a-9d56-3df9c7384c94" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452503} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05cc96c4-9bd0-5ec2-9c67-70c12fe56748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451313} {:answer/id #uuid "71be77cb-f2a0-5da9-a71b-0d54bf22920e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451314}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451313} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fda6962-d111-5d52-9d97-94bb5cf1b992" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452504} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "757e1e41-d4bd-5b02-ae6c-09acb0b026a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451315} {:answer/id #uuid "ae88a715-f3a8-55f4-890a-5a6165ebc0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451316}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451316} :answer-field/id #uuid "c19a4f3d-993f-546f-aa3d-c187f50b1a7a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bb8cc465-a001-5ad5-bfcd-11eb17855f11" :question/math-academy-id "q-140844" :question/problem "Given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is not diagonalizable\n- **II.** $A$ must have $4$ integer eigenvalues\n- **III.** If  $\\alpha $ is an eigenvalue of $A$ with the multiplicity $2,$ then the dimension of the eigenspace for the eigenvalue $\\alpha $ is $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the spectral theorem, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable, so, $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real but not necessarily integers.\n\n- Statement III is true. According to the theorem, the dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "204942ae-e32e-531e-8f95-9aa08d764a99"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544917 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a65a271-e819-5161-9c5f-b4658866c25c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395054} {:answer/id #uuid "3b601ff3-863f-5502-84d6-13f00e2ad45e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395055} {:answer/id #uuid "84da8f15-dc10-55f3-97d6-434ca1ff643e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395056} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb077ea-553a-5aa6-a64a-7a2b84621b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\begin{bmatrix}80 & 60 \\\\ 60 & 45\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395057} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2d006a-6b5c-519d-a0ac-32bc5de9d2ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395058}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395054} :answer-field/id #uuid "3211a6bb-133a-5e40-90d8-fc361c03ff47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "05f92170-0d3f-5d3a-aa67-7dea2e387ec7" :question/math-academy-id "q-95710" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$ Computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=5.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "33991c0d-45f8-5bde-a9e0-865b009ce6cf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544988}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544928 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b36379c9-48d8-5cb1-80f0-4b648453256c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378060} {:answer/id #uuid "600f54d6-7fa1-5cb7-8b0b-961230b3ef4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378061} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc06545-06d9-5a6d-b04a-bdc1e2c72d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378062} {:answer/id #uuid "f04919e1-818b-5e94-8009-e3cff20a8da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378063} {:answer/id #uuid "4b6a25f6-1c57-57ce-9ba5-04288080446d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378064}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378061} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6818491-5219-59f7-95e1-4f023133c8d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eab64778-c05e-560d-a0b4-afdd1f4a716c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95706" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5621b370-c250-590a-850c-a09cc9236f08" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544917}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544933 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49409b3e-6cf3-58d5-a30f-981d7b832aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544246} {:answer/id #uuid "5f102541-4e5b-562c-9b94-e08a3d1c19d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544292} {:answer/id #uuid "884bd029-05ca-5b4a-a6a0-982c739c7b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544381} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d80d43-4b97-54e9-b8b5-55a67da752e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544501} {:answer/id #uuid "f98d9e4b-b211-526a-8436-d0f20159b86b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544632}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544632} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1086491-d596-5df5-9f5e-b792d5105671" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eaf43e73-9a1c-5cad-a4f5-266c54b6fab6" :question/math-academy-id "q-95408" :question/problem "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ must have all real eigenvalues\n\nII. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors, then $u⊥v$\n\nIII. $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable" :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal, but two eigenvectors corresponding to the same eigenvalue may not be orthogonal.\nThis can occur when the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvaule is greater than $1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "652a9dac-c9d8-5ab6-956b-06621fefc495" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544928}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544988 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47b8923f-7afa-5e14-b149-d06f52309cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366686} {:answer/id #uuid "02cd176f-d7d8-56e4-9747-5567e658dda6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366687} {:answer/id #uuid "4a0c2bac-5abf-5014-936e-ed7fb7b084ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366688} {:answer/id #uuid "970025c4-617f-501a-a55b-b16b187ad5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366689} {:answer/id #uuid "e3545f21-098a-50f3-a494-4ea653f16e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366690}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366690} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb8b9480-d64b-50e7-a54e-52af09ad2e90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "61a2391d-9048-50a5-89de-abedafa32ffa" :question/math-academy-id "q-95720" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eab4f6a7-357a-59d5-b5d9-5cb4b9754a49" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544908}}] :activity/title "The Spectral Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544069}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120720} :activity/id #uuid "87481402-fa01-553f-b61a-d34f3d67f347" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050148 :topic/id #uuid "30d88a85-9b00-5b6d-90cc-41ac3f6c9da8" :topic/math-academy-id 1808} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120720 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121053 :tutorial/content "Consider the following equations of a line $l$ and a plane $\\Pi\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nl: \\mathbf{r} = \\langle 2,2,1 \\rangle + t \\langle 3,0,1 \\rangle, \\qquad \\Pi:\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 3,-5,1 \\rangle = 7.\n$$\n\nHow can we find the intersection point $P$ of $l$ and $\\Pi?$\n\nWe start by writing the equation of the line in the form $\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 2+3t , 2, 1+t \\rangle.$ Now, since $P$ must lie on the line $l,$ we can write its coordinates as $P(2+3t , 2, 1+t)$ for some particular value of $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n![](images/d3/d3ba164eb98a53c463744e11d6593177ddde2a0a309f752e5d86b30db0d79925.png)\n\nOn the other hand, $P$ also lies on the plane $\\Pi.$ So, the coordinates $P(2+3t , 2, 1+t)$ must satisfy the equation of the plane as well. Substituting the position vector $\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 2+3t , 2, 1+t \\rangle$ into the equation of the plane and solving for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,1⟩ & =7 \\\\ ⟨2+3t,2,1+t⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-5,1⟩ & =7 \\\\ 3(2+3t)-5\\cdot 2+(1+t) & =7 \\\\ 6+9t-10+1+t & =7 \\\\ 10t & =10 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $\\mathbf{p}$ of the intersection point $P,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨2+3(1),2,1+(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $P(5, 2, 2).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b2ca8cb0-0b92-5b99-ac8b-edd2f2a6dbb1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3259 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a814365d-ddd3-5356-ac6d-30d046db60e2" :step/math-academy-id 17931 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120721}} {:db/id